title Radiation
date 29/09/2026

Q: Intuitive approach of the field created by a moving charge
	A: When a charge moves, its field get offset by a certain amount inside a sphere of radius $ct$. At the border of this sphere, the inner field meets the outer one, and the connection of the field lines generates a transverse field over the sphere. The filed lines in the direction of the motion of the charge remain unchanged – no radiation in that direction.

## Green’s functions – retarded potentials and fields

Q: Green’s function associated with the differential linear operator $\mathscr{L}$ and how to use it to solve an equation $\mathscr{L}(x)\big(u(x)\big) = f(x)$
	A: It is the solution $G$ of the equation
		math
			\mathscr{L}(\r)\big(G(\r, \r')\big) = δ(\r-\r')
		To solve the equation, we just add these Green’s functions :
		math
			u(x) = ∫_{-∞}^{+∞}f(x')G(x, x')\dd x'
		satisfies
		math
			\begin{align*}
			\mathscr{L}(x)\big(u(x)\big) &= ∫_{-∞}^{+∞}f(x')\mathscr{L}(x) \big(G(x, x')\big)\dd x'\\
			&= ∫_{-∞}^{+∞}f(x') δ(x-x')\dd x'\\
			&= f(x)
			\end{align*}

Q: Green’s function vanishing at infinity associated with the Laplacian
	A: math
			G(\r, \r') = \frac{1}{-4 π}\frac{1}{\abs{\r - \r'}}
		In fact,
		math
			\lapl_{\r} \left(\frac{1}{-4 π}\frac{1}{\abs{\r - \r'}}\right) = δ(\r - \r')

Q: Green’s function of the operator $-\lapl + \frac{1}{c^{2}}\pdvN{}{t}{2}$ if no field is present when the source is turned on and if the fields created vanish at infinity (retadred Green’s function)
	A: For a wave created at $\r, t$ in response to an impulsive excitation at point $\r'$ at time $t'$,
		math
			G^{\text{ret}} (\r-\r', t-t') = \frac{1}{4 π \abs{\r-\r'}} δ \left(t - t' - \frac{\abs{\r - \r'}}{c}\right)

Q: Retarded potentials
	A: By applying the retarded Green’s function, we find :
		math
			ϕ(\r, t) = \frac{1}{4 π ε_{0}}\iiint_{\R^{3}}ρ \left(\r', t - \frac{\abs{\r - \r'}}{c}\right)\frac{1}{\abs{\r - \r'}}\dd^{3}\r'
		and
		math
			\A(\r, t) = \frac{μ_{0}}{4 π}\iiint_{\R^{3}}\j \left(\r', t - \frac{\abs{\r - \r'}}{c}\right)\frac{1}{\abs{\r-\r'}}\dd^{3}\r'
		At time $t$, they do not depend on the state of the sources at that time but on their state at an erlier instant $t - \frac{\abs{\r - \r'}}{c}$.

## Application: oscillating dipole

Q: Definition of the electric dipole
	A:
		math
			\vec{p}(t) = \int \r ρ(\r, t)\dd V

Q: Charge density of a point dipole
	A: If a charge $+q$ is located at $\r + \vec{d}/2$ and a $-q$ charge is located at $\r - \vec{d}/2$, then
		math
			ρ(\r) = q δ \left(\r - \frac{\vec{d}}{2}\right) - q δ \left(\r + \frac{\vec{d}}{2}\right)
		but in the point dipole approximation, we have $d\to 0$. We can thus use the first-order expansion $δ(\r \pm \vec{a}) = δ(\r)\pm \vec{a} · \grad δ(\r) + O(a^{2})$ to obtain
		math
			ρ(\r) = - \vec{p} ·  \grad δ(\r)

Q: Current density of a point dipole
	A: $\j(\r, t) = q\dot{\r_{+}}δ(\r - \r_{+}) - q\dot{\r_{-}}δ(\r-\r_{-})$ with $\r_{\pm} = \pm \vec{d}/2$ and $d \to 0$ gives
		math
			\j(\r, t) = +\dot{\p}(t)δ(\r)

Q: Three regions of space for an oscillating dipole
	A: Let $τ$ be a characteristic time of the source (for example $τ = 1/f$).
		- ++near field++ ($r \small c τ$) : dominated by the $1/r^{3}$ terms, structure of the field of a static dipole (reactive energy)
		- ++intermediate field++ ($r \simeq c τ$) : $1/r^{2}$ terms proportional to $\dot{\vec{p}}(t)$ (reactive energy)
		- ++far field++ ($l \large c τ$) : $1/r$ terms proportional to the acceleration $\ddot{\vec{p}}(t)$ (radiation)

Q: Total electric field of an oscillating dipole
	A: For a point dipole oscillating parallel to $\uz$, we have
		math
			\E(\r, t) = \underbrace{\left(\frac{2\cos θ}{4 π ε_{0}}\ur + \frac{\sin θ}{4 π ε_{0}}\uth\right)}_{\text{static field lines}}\underbrace{\left(\frac{p_{\text{ret}}}{r^{3}} + \frac{\dot{p}_{\text{ret}}}{cr^{2}}\right)}_{\text{amplitude at $t_{\text{ret}}$}} + \underbrace{\frac{\sin θ}{4 π ε_{0}} \left(\frac{\ddot{p}_{\text{ret}}}{c^{2}r}\right)\uth}_{\text{transverse field}}
		==picture slide 33/117==

Q: Fields radiated by a dipole $p_{0}e^{-i ωt}\uz$
	A: The triad $(\ur, \E, \B)$ is right-handed :
		math
			\B(\r, t) = -\frac{μ_{0}}{4πc}p_{0}ω^{2}\sin θ \frac{1}{r} e^{-i ω \left(t - \frac{r}{c}\right)}\uph
		math
			\E(\r, t) = -\frac{1}{4πε_{0}c^{2}}p_{0}ω^{2}\sin θ \frac{1}{r} e^{-i ω \left(t - \frac{r}{c}\right)}\uth
		The radiated wave locally has the structure of a *plane wave*.
		==picture slide 36/117==

Q: Poynting vector of the far field of a point dipole $p_{0}e^{-i ω t}\uz$
	A: math
			\moy{\poynting} = \frac{1}{32 π^{2}ε_{0}c^{3}}\left(\frac{p_{0}ω^{2}\sin θ}{r}\right)^{2}\ur
		- $1/r^{2}$ decay : total average power radiated is independant of $r$ at large distance
		- $\sin^{2} θ$ : no radiation in the direction of the dipole
		- $ω^{4}$

Q: Definition — radiation pattern
	A: Graphical representation of the angular distribution $D(θ, φ)$ of a quantity characterizing the radiation of a source (antenna, dipole, …)
		math
			D(θ, φ) = \frac{\moy{\poynting(\r, θ, φ)} · \ur}{P/4 π r^{2}}
		where $P$ is the total radiated power.
		(comparison to a perfectly isothopic source (which does not exist) with the same radiated power)

Q: Radiation pattern of an oscillating dipole
	A: math
			D(θ, φ) = \frac{3}{2}\sin^{2} θ
		==picture slide 39/117==

## Radiation in the general case

Q: Definition of radiation
	A: A source radiates into a solid angle element $\dd Ω$ if $\dd P = \u_{r} · \moy{\poynting(\r, t)}r^{2} \dd Ω$ does not vanish as $r$ tend to infinity.
		This imposes $\poynting ∝ \frac{\ur}{r^{2}}$ and therefore that the fields decay as $1/r$.

Q: Dipole condition: far zone
	A: Usually
		math
			r \large λ
		In practice, the classical Fraunhofer condition is often used :
		math
			r \arge r_{FF} = \frac{2D^{2}}{λ}

Q: Dipole condition: non-relativistic speed
	A: We want $v = O \left(\frac{a}{T}\right) \small c$, i.e.
		math
			a \small λ

Q: Potentials of the radiated (approximations)
	A: With the $r \large a$ approximation,
		math
			\begin{align*}
				\abs{\r - \r'} &= \r \sqrt{1 - 2 \frac{\ur · \r'}{r} + \left(\frac{r'}{r}\right)^{2}}\\
				&\simeq r - \r' · \ur
			\end{align*}
		and thus,
		math
			φ_{\text{ray}}(\r, t) = \frac{1}{4 π ε_{0}r}\iiint ρ\left(\r', t - \frac{r}{c} + \frac{\r' · \ur}{c}\right)\dd^{3}\r'
		and
		math
			\A_{\text{ray}}(\r, t) = \frac{μ_{0}}{4 π r}\iiint \j\left(\r', t - \frac{r}{c} + \frac{\r' · \ur}{c}\right)\dd^{3}\r'

Q: Radiated field (with approximations)
	A: By using
		math
			\nabla_{r}\left(t - \frac{r}{c} + \frac{\r' · \ur}{c}\right) = -\frac{\ur}{c} - \frac{\ur}{c}∧  \left(\ur ∧ \frac{\r'}{r}\right) \simeq - \frac{\ur}{c}
		we get (using the chain rule on $\j$) :
		math
			\B_{\text{ray}}(\r, t) = - \frac{\ur}{c} ∧ \pdv{\A_{\text{ray}}(\r, t)}{t}
		and
		math
			\E_{\text{ray}}(\r, t) = -\ur ∧ c\B_{\text{ray}}(\r, t)
		$(\ur, \E_{\text{ray}}, \B_{\text{ray}})$ form a right-handed triad.

Q: Vector potential of a radiated field in the fourier domain (with approximations)
	A: In the time domain,
		math
			\A_{\text{ray}} (\r, t) = \frac{μ_{0}}{4 πr}\iiint \j \left(\r', t - \frac{r}{c} + \frac{\r' · \ur}{c}\right)\dd^{3}\r'
		Introducing $\k = \frac{ω}{c}\ur$ and taking as time dependence $e^{-i ωt}$, we obtain
		math
			\A_{\text{ray}}(\r, ω) = \frac{μ_{0}}{4 π} \frac{e^{ikr}}{r} \iiint \j(\r', ω) e^{-i \k · \r'}\dd^{3}\r'
		($\j(\r', t) = \j(\r', ω)e^{-i ωt}$ and $\A(\r, t) = \A(\r, ω)e^{-i ωt}$).
		So by writing $\tilde{\j}(\vec{K}, ω) \defeq \iiint \j(\r', ω)e^{-i \vec{K}· \r'}\dd^{3}\r'$ the spatial (inverse) Fourrier transform of $\j(\r, ω)$, we get
		math
			\A_{\text{ray}}(\r, ω) = \frac{μ_{0}}{4 π}\frac{e^{ikr}}{r}\tilde{\j(k\ur, ω)}
		math
			\B_{\text{ray}}(\r, ω) = \frac{i ω\ur}{c}∧ \A_{\text{ray}}(\r, ω)
		math
			\E_{\text{ray}}(\r, ω) = -i ω\ur ∧ \big(\ur ∧ \A_{\text{ray}}(\r, ω)\big)
		Furthermore, if $\vec{p}(t) = \vec{p}_{0}e^{-i ωt}$, we have as $kr' = 2 π r'/λ \small 1$, $e^{-i \k · \r'} \simeq 1 - i\k · \r'$ thus, keeping only the dominant term :
		math
			\A_{\text{ray}}(\r, ω) = - \frac{μ_{0}}{4 π}\frac{e^{ikr}}{r}i ω \vec{p}
		as $\j(\r, t) = \dot{\vec{p}}(t)δ(\r)$ or in the harmonic regime $\j(\r, ω) = -i ω \vec{p}_{0}$. (expression still valid for an arbitrary charge distribution if its dimension is small compared to $λ$. Just take the dipole moment of the distribution.)

Q: Radiated power on the frequency domain
	A: It only depends on the transverse part of the spatial transform of the current :
		math
			\moy{\dv{P}{Ω}} = \frac{μ_{0}ω^{2}}{32 π^{2}c} \abs{\ur ∧ \tilde{\j}(k\ur, ω)}^{2}

